# =============================================================================
# VenueRise — Environment Variables
# Copy this file to .env.local and fill in real values. Never commit .env.local.
#
# Phase 7A note — for production deploys, every variable in the following
# REQUIRED-IN-PRODUCTION set must be present, or `/api/readiness` returns 503:
#
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY
#   SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY
#   ANTHROPIC_API_KEY
#   INTERNAL_API_SECRET            (≥ 32 chars; `openssl rand -hex 32`)
#   INNGEST_EVENT_KEY
#   INNGEST_SIGNING_KEY
#   RESEND_API_KEY
#   RESEND_FROM_EMAIL
#   RESEND_WEBHOOK_SECRET
#   UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_URL
#   UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_TOKEN
#   SENTRY_DSN
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN
#
# Run `npm run check:prod-env` against a populated env to verify locally.
# See docs/DEPLOYMENT.md for end-to-end provisioning steps and docs/SECURITY.md
# for the trust-boundary model.
# =============================================================================

# Supabase — public (safe to expose to the browser)
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project.supabase.co
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=eyJ...

# Supabase — service role. SERVER-ONLY. Never prefix with NEXT_PUBLIC_.
# Found in: Supabase dashboard → Project Settings → API → service_role key.
# Note (Phase 6A): tenant access now flows through `venue_members` + roles.
# The legacy `venues.owner_user_id` column is still honored as a fallback
# so anything pre-migration-004 keeps working. See lib/auth/tenant-access.ts.
# RLS now honors venue_members as of migration 005 — non-owner members
# (sales_manager / coordinator / viewer) can read tenant rows directly via
# user-scoped queries; write routes are role-gated via requireVenueRole.
# Phase 6C: new authenticated users provision their workspace via
# POST /api/onboarding/create-workspace (no manual Supabase SQL required).
# Phase 6D: owners/admins invite teammates via POST /api/team/invitations.
# Invitation emails use NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL to build the accept link
# (`${APP_URL}/onboarding/accept?token=...`). If Resend is not configured,
# the email body is logged to stdout but the invite row + token are still
# created — share the URL out-of-band.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=eyJ...

# Anthropic API. SERVER-ONLY.
# Get from: https://console.anthropic.com/settings/keys
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-...
# Anthropic calls are wrapped in a retry helper (Phase 5D): transient 429/5xx
# errors retry up to 3 times with exponential backoff + jitter; each attempt
# is bounded by a 30s AbortController timeout. Terminal failures are captured
# in Sentry and logged as `ai.anthropic.failed`.

# Base URL of this app. Used by /api/widget to call /api/ai/qualify internally.
# In production this should be the canonical https URL (no trailing slash).
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL=http://localhost:3000

# =============================================================================
# Internal HMAC secret — legacy from Phase 1. Still wired in case the job
# queue is rolled back, but no longer the primary trigger path. SERVER-ONLY.
# Generate: `openssl rand -hex 32` (min 32 chars).
# =============================================================================
INTERNAL_API_SECRET=replace-me-with-openssl-rand-hex-32

# =============================================================================
# Inngest — job/workflow runtime (Phase 2)
#
# LOCAL DEV (recommended):
#   1. `npx inngest-cli@latest dev` in a separate terminal — it autodetects
#      http://localhost:3000/api/inngest and runs all event + cron functions
#      locally. NO keys needed.
#   2. Set INNGEST_DEV=1 so the app's queue abstraction routes through Inngest
#      instead of the in-process setImmediate fallback. Optional but recommended
#      if you want to test cron behavior locally.
#
# PRODUCTION:
#   Both INNGEST_EVENT_KEY and INNGEST_SIGNING_KEY must be set or events are
#   silently dropped. Pulled from https://app.inngest.com → your app → Keys.
# =============================================================================
# INNGEST_DEV=1
# INNGEST_EVENT_KEY=
# INNGEST_SIGNING_KEY=

# =============================================================================
# Resend — email delivery (Phase 3)
#
# Used by the scheduled follow-up + tour-reminder jobs to actually send mail.
#
# PRODUCTION:
#   - RESEND_API_KEY is required.
#   - RESEND_FROM_EMAIL must use a DOMAIN you have verified at
#     https://resend.com/domains — otherwise Resend rejects every send.
#     Example: notifications@mail.yourvenue.com
#   - RESEND_REPLY_TO_EMAIL is optional but recommended so leads' replies
#     land in a human-monitored mailbox (e.g. coordinator@yourvenue.com).
#
# DEVELOPMENT:
#   - If RESEND_API_KEY or RESEND_FROM_EMAIL are absent, the app runs in
#     CONSOLE-FALLBACK mode: the generated email is logged to the server
#     console and NOT delivered to anyone. Follow-up rows flip to status
#     'skipped' (not 'sent') so we never falsely claim delivery. Tour
#     reminder flags are NOT flipped — next scan retries once Resend is
#     configured.
# =============================================================================
# RESEND_API_KEY=re_...
# RESEND_FROM_EMAIL=notifications@mail.yourvenue.com
# RESEND_REPLY_TO_EMAIL=coordinator@yourvenue.com

# Resend webhook signing secret (Phase 4B).
# Provision in Resend dashboard:
#   Webhooks → Add Endpoint
#     URL:    https://<your-deploy>/api/resend/webhook
#     Events: email.delivered, email.bounced, email.complained,
#             email.delivery_delayed, email.failed
# Copy the 'Signing Secret' (starts with `whsec_...`) into this var.
# REQUIRED in production. Without it, /api/resend/webhook returns 401 to
# every incoming webhook and /api/health reports resend_webhook: 'missing'.
# RESEND_WEBHOOK_SECRET=whsec_...

# =============================================================================
# Upstash Redis — rate limiting (Phase 4A)
#
# Protects the widget, AI routes, and lead-create from abuse.
#
# PRODUCTION:
#   Both vars REQUIRED. Without them, every protected route runs unlimited
#   and `/api/health` returns `upstash: 'missing'` — treat that as a P1 alert.
#
# DEVELOPMENT:
#   Optional. Missing → rate limiting is a no-op (logs ONE warning on first
#   request, then stays silent). `/api/health` returns `upstash: 'disabled'`.
#
# Provision:
#   https://console.upstash.com → Create Redis (Global, eventual consistency
#   is fine for sliding-window counters) → REST URL + REST token.
# =============================================================================
# UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_URL=https://xxx.upstash.io
# UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_TOKEN=...

# =============================================================================
# Sentry — error observability (Phase 5C)
#
# Without SENTRY_DSN the app runs normally and `/api/health` reports
# `sentry: 'missing'`. Capture helpers (captureApiError / captureJobError /
# captureWebhookError / captureAiError) become no-ops.
#
# In production set:
#   SENTRY_DSN              — runtime DSN (server + edge SDK use this)
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN  — same value, exposed to the browser bundle
#                             (Sentry never auto-redacts public DSNs — by
#                             design; they're meant to be exposed)
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_VERSION — surfaces in /api/health and Sentry release tags
#                             (falls back to package.json#version)
#   SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN       — BUILD-TIME ONLY, for source map upload via
#                             the Sentry webpack/turbopack plugin. Not used
#                             at runtime in Phase 5C; kept for future use.
#
# Trace sampling:
#   SENTRY_TRACES               — server (default 0.1)
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_TRACES   — client (default 0.01)
#
# Debugging:
#   SENTRY_DEBUG=1 — verbose Sentry SDK output to console
# =============================================================================
# SENTRY_DSN=https://xxx@oXXXX.ingest.sentry.io/XXXX
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN=https://xxx@oXXXX.ingest.sentry.io/XXXX
# NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_VERSION=0.1.0
# SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN=
# SENTRY_TRACES=0.1
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_TRACES=0.01

# =============================================================================
# Request correlation (Phase 5B) — no env var required.
#
# Clients and proxies MAY send `X-Request-Id` on inbound requests. If present
# and well-formed (max 128 chars, [A-Za-z0-9_-.:]), VenueRise reuses it. If
# missing or invalid, VenueRise generates a UUIDv4. The id is:
#   - attached to every log line in the request scope
#   - propagated into job event payloads (request_id)
#   - returned as `X-Request-Id` on every response
# =============================================================================

# =============================================================================
# Logging (Phase 5A) — pino structured logs
#
# LOG_LEVEL: trace | debug | info | warn | error | fatal | silent
#   Default: 'debug' in development, 'info' in production.
#
# Logs are JSON to stdout. For pretty local logs, pipe:
#   npm run dev | npx pino-pretty
# =============================================================================
# LOG_LEVEL=info

# =============================================================================
# Stripe Billing (Phase 7C)
#
# All three are REQUIRED in production — /api/readiness returns 503 if any
# are missing. In local dev they're optional: the app boots, /api/health
# reports billing.stripe="missing", and any call to /api/billing/* returns
# 503 billing_not_configured.
#
# PROVISION (see docs/DEPLOYMENT.md for the full walk-through):
#   1. Create a Stripe account; switch to Test mode.
#   2. Dashboard → Developers → API Keys:
#        - Copy the "Secret key" (sk_test_…)            → STRIPE_SECRET_KEY
#        - Copy the "Publishable key" (pk_test_…)       → NEXT_PUBLIC_STRIPE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY
#   3. Dashboard → Product Catalog → New product → New recurring price.
#        - Copy the price id (price_…)                  → STRIPE_DEFAULT_PRICE_ID
#   4. AFTER first deploy: Dashboard → Developers → Webhooks → Add endpoint:
#        - URL: https://<your-deploy>/api/stripe/webhook
#        - Events: checkout.session.completed,
#                  customer.subscription.{created,updated,deleted},
#                  invoice.payment_{succeeded,failed}
#        - Copy the signing secret (whsec_…)            → STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET
#
# WARNING: never ship live (sk_live_…) keys to staging. Match the key prefix
# to the environment — readiness logs the mode in checkStripe() debug.
# =============================================================================
# STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=sk_test_...
# STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=whsec_...
# STRIPE_DEFAULT_PRICE_ID=price_...
# NEXT_PUBLIC_STRIPE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=pk_test_...   # publishable; safe to expose. Not used until Phase 7D.
# STRIPE_API_VERSION=                              # optional override; defaults to SDK's compiled-in version
#
# Phase 7F note: Stripe webhook events are persisted to
# `public.billing_events_log` (migration 008) for forensic / audit access.
# No additional env vars required — the audit log is on by default once
# migration 008 has been applied. See docs/BILLING-QA.md §7a.
#
# Phase 7G note: owner/admin operators can inspect the audit log via
# `GET /api/admin/billing-events` (list, payload omitted) and
# `GET /api/admin/billing-events/[id]` (single row, payload included).
# No env required. See docs/BILLING-QA.md §7b.
#
# Phase 7H note: a daily Inngest cron `billing-trial-reminder` (schedule
# `0 14 * * *`) emails venue owners 3 days before their `trial_end`.
# Uses existing INNGEST_* + RESEND_* + Supabase env vars — no additional
# secret required. Idempotency lives on `subscriptions.metadata.reminders_sent`.
# See docs/BILLING-QA.md §7c.
#
# Phase 7I note: owner/admin operators can replay a failed Stripe event via
# `POST /api/admin/billing-events/[id]/replay`. Reuses STRIPE_SECRET_KEY +
# the billing_events_log audit table (migration 008). No new env vars.
# See docs/BILLING-QA.md §7d.
#
# Phase 7J note: migration 009 extends billing_events_log with replay audit
# columns (`replayed_at`, `replayed_by`, `replay_count`) and a SECURITY
# DEFINER RPC `public.record_billing_event_replay(uuid, uuid)`. No env vars.
# See docs/BILLING-QA.md §7d "Replay attribution" and docs/SECURITY.md §10f.
#
# Phase 7K note: a daily Inngest cron `billing-dunning` (schedule `0 16 * * *`)
# emails the venue owner when their subscription is `past_due`. Stops after 3
# attempts per current_period_end; respects a 48h spacing window between
# attempts. Uses existing INNGEST_*, STRIPE_*, and RESEND_* env vars — no
# additional secret required. Idempotency lives on
# `subscriptions.metadata.dunning_sent`. See docs/BILLING-QA.md §7e.
#
# Phase 7L note: migration 010 adds the atomic JSONB array append RPC
# `public.append_subscription_metadata_array(uuid, text, jsonb)`. Both
# billing crons (trial reminder, dunning) now write metadata through this
# RPC instead of a read-modify-write. Closes the race with the Stripe
# webhook subscription sync. Service-role-only; uses the existing
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY. See docs/BILLING-QA.md §7g.
#
# Phase 7M note: a single "payment recovered" email fires from the Stripe
# webhook path when a subscription transitions past_due → active|trialing.
# No cron, no new env vars — reuses STRIPE_*, RESEND_*, and the
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY. Idempotent on
# `subscriptions.metadata.recovery_sent`. See docs/BILLING-QA.md §7h.
#
# Phase 7N note: operators can clear stale dunning attempts via
# `POST /api/admin/billing-events/[id]/clear-dunning`. Backed by
# migration 011's RPC `public.remove_subscription_metadata_array_entries`.
# Reuses admin auth + audit log + service-role infrastructure — no new
# env vars. See docs/BILLING-QA.md §7i and docs/RUNBOOK.md §2.4i.

# =============================================================================
# Phase 8A — demo seed/reset script env vars (NOT Vercel runtime vars).
# Read by scripts/seed-demo-venue.mjs + scripts/reset-demo-venue.mjs when
# you run them from a laptop or CI runner against a deployed app. Do NOT
# set them in Vercel — they have no runtime effect there.
#
# scripts/seed-demo-venue.mjs — wipes + inserts ~10 leads + 4 conversations
# + 4 tours + 7 follow-ups + 5 ai_actions for the test user's primary
# venue. Idempotent. Demo rows tagged so reset can target only its own data.
#
# scripts/reset-demo-venue.mjs — delete-only path. Removes leads matching
# 'demo+%@venuerise.test' and ai_actions with agent='demo-seed'. Real
# data untouched.
# DEMO_APP_URL=http://localhost:3000
# DEMO_SUPABASE_URL=https://xxx.supabase.co
# DEMO_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=eyJ...
# DEMO_TEST_USER_EMAIL=owner@example.com
# DEMO_TEST_USER_PASSWORD=replace-me

# =============================================================================
# Phase 8B — public flag that toggles the dashboard "Send test inquiry"
# button on the /dashboard/leads page header. Public env var, safe to expose.
#
# Set to "1" locally or in staging for founder-led demos. Keep DISABLED
# (unset or anything other than "1") in production unless you're
# intentionally demoing — the button fires a real /api/widget POST that
# creates a lead + triggers the AI orchestration.
#
# Realtime layers on /dashboard/leads and /dashboard/inbox are always
# mounted (zero env flag needed); they only do anything when Supabase
# Realtime + the publication on leads/conversations is configured (already
# set up by migration 001).
#
# Phase 8C: the same flag gates the variant inquiry picker (Garden /
# Greenhouse / All-inclusive package). No new env vars. Quick-schedule-tour
# action lives inside the LeadDetailPanel and respects ADMIN/SALES role
# gating + Phase 7D billing gate at the API layer; no client-side flag.
# Realtime tours refresh on /dashboard/tours is also always mounted.
#
# Phase 8D: NEXT_PUBLIC_DEMO_BUTTON=1 now ONLY controls the quick demo
# shortcuts (variant inquiry button on /dashboard/leads + QuickScheduleTourButton
# inside the LeadDetailPanel). The full ScheduleTourDrawer is always
# available to authenticated SALES_ROLES via the "Schedule Tour" CTA on
# /dashboard/tours and the "Schedule tour…" button in every lead detail
# panel — no flag needed. Drawer is role-gated + billing-gated at the
# /api/tours POST.
#
# Phase 8E: tour edit/cancel/Mark-confirmed + URL-based month navigation
# on /dashboard/tours (`?month=YYYY-MM`) are production UX. No demo flag
# required. NEXT_PUBLIC_DEMO_BUTTON continues to control only the demo
# shortcuts from earlier phases.
#
# Phase 8F: TourLifecycleStrip on /dashboard/inbox/[leadId],
# POST /api/admin/tours/bulk-cancel, and the `billing-tour-auto-pause`
# Inngest cron (daily 6pm UTC, 7-day grace on past_due venues) all run
# without any new env vars. The cron uses the existing service-role
# Supabase client; the admin route uses the existing requireAdmin +
# rate-limit stack. No NEXT_PUBLIC_DEMO_BUTTON gating either — the
# inbox strip is production UX, available to anyone who can see the
# inbox.
#
# Phase 8G: Auto-unpause metadata on payment recovery
# (`tours_resumed_at` + `tours_resumed_reason`), the
# GET /api/admin/tours/paused-venues admin endpoint, lead-facing tour
# notification emails (created/rescheduled/confirmed/cancelled), and the
# /dashboard/tours auto-pause banner all run without any new env vars.
# Notification email delivery rides on the existing Resend configuration
# (RESEND_API_KEY + RESEND_FROM_EMAIL); when those are absent, the email
# layer falls back to console logging in development and silently logs
# misses in production — tour writes always succeed regardless.
#
# Phase 8I: Tour pause audit surface — adds GET /api/admin/tours/pause-history
# and POST /api/admin/tours/clear-pause, the PauseHistoryTable on
# /dashboard/settings/billing (admins+owners only — RLS-gated by the
# existing subscriptions ADMIN_ROLES policy), and a "Re-schedule cancelled
# tour" shortcut on the inbox TourLifecycleStrip. No new env vars. Both
# admin routes use the existing requireAdmin() + rate-limit stack and the
# clear-pause endpoint preserves `tour_pause_history` while stamping
# `tours_pause_cleared_{at,by,reason}` for forensic traceability.
#
# Phase 8J: Lead-facing tour reminder template upgrade (24h + 2h reminders
# now route through the shared Phase 8G `buildTourNotificationBody`
# helper so the visual identity matches the create/confirm/cancel emails),
# "This week's tours" sidebar panel on /dashboard/inbox, and a new admin
# endpoint GET /api/admin/tours/notification-stats for delivery rate
# triage. No new env vars; depends on the existing Resend + Supabase +
# admin auth setup. Reminder delivery still requires RESEND_API_KEY +
# RESEND_FROM_EMAIL — when those are absent, the cron continues to
# console-fallback without flipping the reminder flag (Phase 3 behavior
# preserved).
#
# Phase 8K: Lead-facing tour confirm/cancel links. Adds public
# GET /tour/confirm + GET /tour/cancel (HMAC-signed token in query
# string; no cookie / session / CSRF — the token IS the auth), a new
# admin endpoint GET /api/admin/tours/recent-token-actions for operator
# convenience, and embeds "Confirm your tour" / "Need to cancel?" lines
# in the four lead-facing tour emails (created / rescheduled /
# reminder_24h / reminder_2h). Requires `TOUR_ACTION_SECRET` below; if
# unset the emails still send WITHOUT links and a structured warn fires
# once per process. Per-IP rate limit is reused from the existing
# Upstash user-action limiter (30/min per IP) so accidentally loud
# email blasts don't blow past it.

# =============================================================================
# Phase 8K — TOUR_ACTION_SECRET (server-only, REQUIRED for action links).
#
# HMAC secret used to sign + verify the short-lived (7-day) tokens
# embedded in lead-facing tour emails. Without this var:
#   - emails still send (just no Confirm/Cancel lines)
#   - /tour/confirm + /tour/cancel return "link not valid" pages
#   - a single structured warn fires once per process on first email send
#
# Generate a fresh value:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
#
# Minimum length enforced at 16 chars — anything shorter is treated as
# missing so a placeholder demo value can't accidentally ship to prod.
# =============================================================================
# TOUR_ACTION_SECRET=

# =============================================================================
# Phase 8L — tour action audit + single-use enforcement.
#
# Migration 012 introduced `public.tour_action_events`, an append-only
# audit table that records every redeemed tour action token. Two
# behavioral changes for operators:
#
#   1. Single-use claim — `unique (tour_id, token_nonce)` atomically
#      blocks replay attacks. A token clicked twice flips the tour once
#      and then no-ops with "Already handled" on subsequent clicks.
#   2. Real audit feed — `GET /api/admin/tours/recent-token-actions`
#      now reads from this table (was previously inferring from
#      `tours.updated_at`). Response is unchanged shape-wise.
#
# Also in 8L: lead-facing tour emails now ship multipart with a polished
# HTML body (white card, brand-blue Confirm CTA, muted Cancel link).
# Plaintext stays as the canonical fallback. No env vars required for
# the HTML path.
#
# Optional: TOUR_ACTION_DEBUG_PREVIEW=1 enables the `?as=html&kind=...`
# debug preview on /tour/confirm + /tour/cancel in production. Useful
# for QAing the action page rendering against the real deploy without
# burning real tokens. ALWAYS available in development regardless of
# this flag (NODE_ENV !== 'production' is enough). The preview branch
# reads zero data and mutates nothing.
# =============================================================================
# TOUR_ACTION_DEBUG_PREVIEW=0

# =============================================================================
# Phase 8M — unified tour status-event audit.
#
# Migration 013 introduces `public.tour_status_events`, a unified audit
# feed for EVERY tour status change regardless of write path:
#   - lead token   (/tour/confirm, /tour/cancel)
#   - operator     (PATCH /api/tours/[id])
#   - operator     (POST /api/admin/tours/bulk-cancel)
#   - cron         (billing-tour-auto-pause)
#   - system       (future manual SQL or one-off scripts)
#
# Sits alongside Phase 8L's `tour_action_events` (which keeps the
# single-use lead-token claim via `unique (tour_id, token_nonce)`).
# Lead-token actions write to BOTH tables for one release cycle.
#
# New admin endpoint:
#   GET /api/admin/tours/status-events
#     ?venue_id   (cross-tenant guarded)
#     ?tour_id    (one tour's complete history)
#     ?lead_id    (one lead's audit slice)
#     ?actor_kind (lead_token | operator | cron | system | all)
#     ?action     (exact verb match)
#     ?limit      (1..200, default 50)
#
# The Phase 8K/8L narrow `/api/admin/tours/recent-token-actions` stays
# mounted with `Deprecation: true` + `Link: …status-events?actor_kind=
# lead_token; rel="successor-version"` headers for one release cycle.
#
# No new env vars.
# =============================================================================

# =============================================================================
# Phase 8N — Tour status audit UI + optional one-shot backfill.
#
# UI side: TourAuditDrawer, inbox TourLifecycleStrip recent activity
# panel, and TourStatusActivityFeed (billing settings) all read from the
# Phase 8M `tour_status_events` table via either `/api/admin/tours/status-events`
# (client surfaces) or the service-role client (the billing page server
# fetch). Non-admin operators see 401/403 on the API and the UI silently
# hides the panels — no env config needed.
#
# Optional one-shot backfill: writes one synthetic `actor_kind='system'`
# row per legacy tour so the new audit surfaces always have something to
# show. Disabled by default; the Inngest function `seed-tour-status-events`
# checks this flag at run start and skips with `{ skipped: true,
# reason: 'disabled' }` when not set to "1". The function ALSO requires
# a manual event send (`admin/tour-status-events.backfill`) — there is
# no cron schedule. Two guards = two operator opt-ins.
# =============================================================================
# TOUR_STATUS_BACKFILL=0

# =============================================================================
# Phase 8O — Operator audit feed filters + CSV export + realtime.
#
# No new env vars. Behavior summary:
#
#   1. Billing-page activity feed now has actor_kind + action filter
#      selects on the client. Filters narrow the loaded 25-row slice;
#      use the admin CSV export below for a wider window.
#
#   2. GET /api/admin/tours/status-events?format=csv returns a UTF-8 CSV
#      with Content-Disposition attachment. All other query params
#      (venue_id, tour_id, lead_id, actor_kind, action, limit) work
#      identically. Columns: id, venue_id, tour_id, lead_id, actor_kind,
#      actor_id, action, previous_status, new_status, source_ip,
#      user_agent, reason, occurred_at, metadata_json (compact JSON of
#      the row's metadata jsonb). token_nonce + tour_action_events
#      columns are NEVER included.
#
#   3. RealtimeTourStatusLayer is mounted on /dashboard/tours and
#      /dashboard/settings/billing (admins/owners only on billing). It
#      subscribes to public.tour_status_events INSERTs filtered by
#      venue_id and calls router.refresh() on each event, surfacing
#      a "Tour activity recorded" toast.
#
#      PREREQ: tour_status_events must be in the supabase_realtime
#      publication. Applied via ops command in Phase 8O:
#
#        alter publication supabase_realtime
#        add table public.tour_status_events;
#
#      Re-apply (idempotent) if the table was ever removed — see RUNBOOK
#      §7 "Tour status realtime publication".
# =============================================================================

# =============================================================================
# Phase 8P — URL-synced filters, paginated CSV/JSON export, debounced
# realtime, and audit-drawer deep linking.
#
# No new env vars. Behavior summary:
#
#   1. Billing-page filters now read/write `?actor=<kind>&action=<verb>`.
#      Reset removes both params. Invalid values silently coerce to all.
#
#   2. GET /api/admin/tours/status-events accepts `?occurred_before=<ISO>`
#      for descending cursor pagination. Both JSON and CSV paths honor
#      it. Response shape additions:
#        - JSON: { items, next_cursor, has_more }
#        - CSV : response headers X-Next-Cursor + X-Has-More
#      `next_cursor` is the last row's `occurred_at` when the page is
#      full; chain by passing that value as the next request's
#      `?occurred_before`. The query uses strict `<` so chained pages
#      never duplicate rows.
#
#   3. RealtimeTourStatusLayer now debounces `router.refresh()` calls
#      via a 1000 ms trailing window. A burst of 20 inserts produces 20
#      toasts but typically 1–3 refreshes. Channel/filter unchanged.
#
#   4. /dashboard/tours Audit button now syncs to `?audit_tour=<uuid>`.
#      Deep-linking a tour opens its drawer on initial render; closing
#      the drawer strips the param while preserving the Phase 8E
#      `?month=YYYY-MM` query.
# =============================================================================

# =============================================================================
# Phase 8Q — Per-user filter persistence, audit search, streamed CSV.
#
# No new env vars. Behavior summary:
#
#   1. Billing-page filter state now persists per-user via localStorage
#      under key `venuerise:tour-status-feed:filters:v1`. Priority on
#      load: URL > localStorage > defaults. Reset clears both URL and
#      storage. Invalid JSON in storage is silently ignored.
#
#   2. New `?q=<string>` query param on /api/admin/tours/status-events.
#      Max 120 chars; trimmed; empty after trim coerces to absent.
#      Server-side `or(...)` clause over scalar columns (reason,
#      actor_id, action, previous_status, new_status). `metadata::text`
#      search is NOT applied server-side (see BILLING-QA §7s for
#      rationale); the billing-page client search ALSO matches
#      stringified metadata over the loaded slice.
#
#   3. New `?stream=1` query param (only meaningful with `format=csv`)
#      returns a single continuous CSV via ReadableStream. Internally
#      pages through results using the existing `occurred_at` cursor;
#      page size = `limit` or 200; hard cap = 5000 rows. Headers:
#        Content-Type: text/csv; charset=utf-8
#        Content-Disposition: attachment; filename="tour-status-events-stream-YYYY-MM-DD.csv"
#        X-Streamed: true
#        X-Row-Limit: 5000
#      Honors every other filter (actor_kind, action, q, tour_id,
#      lead_id, venue_id, occurred_before as starting cursor).
# =============================================================================

# =============================================================================
# Phase 8R — Metadata-aware audit search + daily operator digest.
#
# Search side: migration 014 adds the `search_tour_status_events`
# SECURITY DEFINER RPC. The status-events route invokes it ONLY when
# `?q=` is present, which means metadata::text search now works
# server-side. Behavior:
#   - `?q=past_due_7_days` returns rows whose metadata jsonb contains
#     that string.
#   - Same filters, same JSON/CSV/stream shapes — the RPC is a drop-in
#     for the standard PostgREST chain when `q` is set.
#   - RPC is GRANT EXECUTE to service_role only. No direct
#     authenticated/anon access.
#
# Digest side: a new Inngest cron `operator-activity-digest` (daily
# 8am UTC) emails venue owners a 24-hour summary of tour_status_events
# activity. Disabled by default; opt in per environment:
#
# OPERATOR_DIGEST_ENABLED=0
#
# When unset/0, the cron short-circuits with
# `{ skipped: true, reason: 'disabled' }`. When 1, the cron:
#   - aggregates by venue
#   - looks up the venue owner (earliest member with role='owner')
#   - skips owners with no email
#   - probes outbound_messages for today's UTC digest as best-effort
#     idempotency
#   - sends via existing Resend stack
#   - per-venue failures NEVER abort the batch
# =============================================================================
# OPERATOR_DIGEST_ENABLED=0

# =============================================================================
# Phase 8S — Indexed metadata search + digest HTML + unsubscribe.
#
# Server side:
#   - Migration 015 adds pg_trgm + a generated stored column
#     `metadata_text` on tour_status_events + a GIN trigram index. The
#     Phase 8R `search_tour_status_events` RPC swaps its predicate from
#     `metadata::text ilike` to `metadata_text ilike` so the planner
#     can use the index. Operator search latency drops from O(N) row
#     scans to indexed lookups.
#   - The HTTP route contract is unchanged — same query params, same
#     response shapes, same auth posture. `?q=` callers benefit
#     automatically.
#
# Digest side:
#   - The Phase 8R cron now ships an HTML body alongside the plaintext
#     fallback (multipart/alternative via Resend). Outlook-safe table
#     layout, brand-blue total chip, inline CSS only, max-width 480px —
#     mirrors the Phase 8L tour notification visual identity.
#   - Owners can unsubscribe via a one-click HMAC-signed link in the
#     digest body. The opt-out flips
#     `subscriptions.metadata.digest_disabled = true` on the venue's
#     latest subscription row; the cron's per-venue loop checks the
#     flag and skips with `operator_digest.skipped_disabled` before
#     any send work.
#
# Re-enable a venue's digest after opt-out:
#   update public.subscriptions
#   set metadata = (metadata - 'digest_disabled' - 'digest_disabled_at')
#   where id = '<subscription_id>';
# =============================================================================

# =============================================================================
# DIGEST_UNSUBSCRIBE_SECRET (server-only)
#
# HMAC secret used to sign the 30-day unsubscribe tokens embedded in
# the daily operator digest emails. Without this var:
#   - Digest emails still send (just without an unsubscribe link)
#   - GET /api/digest/unsubscribe returns "link not valid" for any token
#   - A single structured warn fires once per process on first digest send
#
# Generate a fresh value:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
#
# Minimum length enforced at 16 chars — anything shorter is treated as
# missing so a placeholder demo value can't accidentally ship to prod.
# =============================================================================
# DIGEST_UNSUBSCRIBE_SECRET=

# =============================================================================
# Phase 8T — Digest cadence preferences + short-query search optimization.
#
# No new env vars. Behavior summary:
#
#   1. Per-venue digest cadence stored at
#      subscriptions.metadata.digest_cadence ∈ 'daily' | 'weekly' | 'off'.
#      - 'daily'  → cron sends every morning (8am UTC).
#      - 'weekly' → cron sends only on Monday UTC.
#      - 'off'   → cron skips (also writes the legacy
#        `digest_disabled = true` flag for back-compat).
#      Missing cadence + no `digest_disabled` defaults to 'daily'.
#
#   2. Admin GET/POST /api/admin/digest/preferences read/write the
#      cadence per venue. Surfaced as a small card on
#      /dashboard/settings/billing for admins/owners.
#
#   3. The Phase 8S unsubscribe route now also writes
#      `digest_cadence = 'off'` so the new model and the legacy
#      `digest_disabled` flag stay coherent across both surfaces.
#
#   4. `/api/admin/tours/status-events?q=` now short-circuits when
#      the search term is shorter than 3 chars — trigram indexes
#      need ≥3 chars to win, so we skip the RPC and use the scalar
#      PostgREST `.or()` chain. Search columns for short queries:
#      actor_id, action, previous_status, new_status, reason,
#      source_ip, user_agent. Metadata search is NOT applied for
#      short terms.
#
#   Existing knobs still apply:
#     - OPERATOR_DIGEST_ENABLED=1 enables the digest cron.
#     - DIGEST_UNSUBSCRIBE_SECRET signs unsubscribe links.
# =============================================================================

# =============================================================================
# Phase 8U — Per-user digest preferences + weekly day-of-week + search hint.
#
# No new env vars. Behavior summary:
#
#   1. Migration 016 adds `venue_members.metadata jsonb` so each
#      admin/owner controls their own digest cadence + weekly day.
#      Effective-preference priority:
#        member.metadata.digest_cadence
#        → subscription.metadata.digest_cadence
#        → subscription.metadata.digest_disabled === true → 'off'
#        → default 'daily'
#
#   2. Weekly cadence now accepts a day-of-week ('sun'..'sat', defaults
#      to 'mon') stored at member or subscription level. Weekly digests
#      fire when the recipient's chosen UTC day matches today.
#
#   3. The Phase 8R cron now fans out to every owner/admin of the
#      venue (capped at MAX_RECIPIENTS_PER_VENUE = 10), with
#      per-recipient idempotency (outbound_messages probe keys on
#      `tour_digest_recipient_user_id`).
#
#   4. The Phase 8K/8S `DIGEST_UNSUBSCRIBE_SECRET`-signed link still
#      writes the venue-level opt-out (subscription.metadata.digest_*).
#      Per-user opt-out happens through the admin UI on Billing
#      Settings; individual admins can re-enable themselves even when
#      the venue-level legacy flag is set.
#
#   5. Billing-page activity feed shows a small amber pill for 1–2
#      char search terms explaining that metadata is excluded until
#      the term reaches 3 chars (the Phase 8T short-circuit threshold).
#
#   Existing knobs still apply:
#     - OPERATOR_DIGEST_ENABLED=1 enables the digest cron.
#     - DIGEST_UNSUBSCRIBE_SECRET signs venue-level unsubscribe links.
# =============================================================================

# =============================================================================
# Phase 8W — Self-serve resubscribe + cron idempotency hardening + preview
# suppression UX.
#
# No new env vars. The resubscribe route reuses DIGEST_UNSUBSCRIBE_SECRET
# (same key as the Phase 8S unsubscribe link). Behavior summary:
#
#   1. New PUBLIC route GET /api/digest/resubscribe?venue_id&user_id&token
#      — per-user counterpart to the Phase 8S venue-level unsubscribe.
#      Verifies an HMAC-signed token carrying `action: 'resubscribe'` +
#      `user_id`, role-gates the membership to owner/admin, and writes
#      `venue_members.metadata.digest_cadence = 'daily'` (plus a
#      `digest_resubscribed_at` audit breadcrumb). Does NOT touch
#      subscriptions.metadata — per the Phase 8U effective-preference
#      resolver, the per-user override wins over the venue-level legacy
#      `digest_disabled` flag, so an individual admin can re-enable
#      themselves regardless of the venue's opt-out state.
#
#   2. Cron + preview rows now carry an explicit
#      `metadata.tour_digest_send_kind` discriminator
#      ('cron' | 'preview', with 'manual' reserved for future operator
#      sends). The cron's per-recipient idempotency probe filters on
#      `send_kind = 'cron'` so a preview clicked earlier today can
#      NEVER block the day's scheduled digest. The Phase 8V
#      `tour_digest_preview = 'true'` marker stays for one release
#      cycle for back-compat with audit queries.
#
#   3. DigestPreferencesCard renders a friendlier amber explanation
#      when POST /api/admin/digest/preview returns 409 `suppressed`
#      (Resend hard-bounce / complaint suppression list). The raw
#      "Couldn't send sample: suppressed" red error is replaced with:
#      "This email address is currently suppressed by our email
#      provider, so we can't send a sample digest to it. Contact
#      support to re-enable delivery for this address."
#
#   Existing knobs still apply:
#     - OPERATOR_DIGEST_ENABLED=1 enables the digest cron.
#     - DIGEST_UNSUBSCRIBE_SECRET signs BOTH unsubscribe and resubscribe
#       links. Rotating invalidates every in-flight token of either kind.
# =============================================================================

# =============================================================================
# Phase 8AF — Audit cursor read + sends-feed URL state + audit event drawer.
#
# One new migration (019), no new env vars, no new admin routes.
# Digest audit rows and digest send rows now support URL-synced
# investigation links.
#
#   1. DigestAuditLogCard reads `?digest_audit_cursor=<ISO>` on
#      mount and threads it into the first fetch. An amber
#      "Viewing an earlier audit page." banner + Jump to latest
#      button surface. Jump to latest clears cursor (in memory +
#      URL); chip / search / Reset also clear it.
#
#   2. DigestAuditFeed mirrors the Phase 8AE URL-state convention.
#      Synced params:
#        ?digest_send_kind
#        ?digest_send_recipient
#        ?digest_send_q          (URL-only; not persisted)
#        ?digest_send_cursor     (write on Load older, read on mount)
#        ?digest_send_archived
#      URL > localStorage > defaults. localStorage keys:
#        venuerise:digest-send-feed:kind:v1
#        venuerise:digest-send-feed:recipient:v1
#        venuerise:digest-send-feed:include-archived:v1
#      Phase 8AC legacy archived key
#      (venuerise:digest-audit-feed:include-archived:v1) still
#      honored for one release cycle. Reset clears every URL
#      param + every storage key (legacy included).
#
#   3. DigestAuditEventDrawer surfaces full row payload when an
#      operator clicks any DigestAuditLogCard row. Pretty-printed
#      metadata JSON via plain <pre>{JSON.stringify(...)}</pre>
#      — no dangerouslySetInnerHTML. Copy audit ID + Copy
#      metadata JSON buttons. When metadata.outbound_message_id is
#      present, a "View related digest send" button sets
#      ?digest_send_q=<id> on the billing page so DigestAuditFeed
#      filters to the matching outbound row.
#
#   4. Migration 019 adds a partial unique index on
#      digest_audit_events:
#        (venue_id, target_user_id, action,
#         (occurred_at at time zone 'utc')::date)
#        WHERE action = 'digest_send_cron'
#      Prevents duplicate digest_send_cron rows from cron retries.
#      recordDigestAuditEvent detects Postgres 23505 + returns
#      { ok: false, error: 'duplicate' } as an info log line; the
#      cron continues. Other actions (suppression / retention /
#      preview) are NOT covered — those legitimately produce
#      multiple rows per (venue, target, day).
#
#   Existing knobs still apply:
#     - DIGEST_AUDIT_LOG_CRON_SENDS=1 logs cron AND preview sends
#       as digest_send_cron / digest_send_preview audit events.
#     - All Phase 8R..8AE digest flags unchanged.
# =============================================================================

# =============================================================================
# Phase 8AE — Audit URL state + metadata search index + preview audit rows.
#
# One new migration (018), no new env vars, no new admin routes.
# Behavior summary:
#
#   1. Migration 018 adds pg_trgm + a generated stored
#      `metadata_text` column on `digest_audit_events` + a GIN
#      trigram index. Mirrors the Phase 8S pattern on
#      `tour_status_events`. Pre-existing rows backfill the column
#      automatically (Postgres re-evaluates the generation
#      expression).
#
#   2. GET /api/admin/digest/audit-events ?q= search now widens to
#      include `metadata_text ILIKE` when the term is ≥ 3 chars
#      (trigram indexes need ≥ 3 chars to win). Below 3 chars stays
#      scalar-only: action / reason / target_email_masked.
#      Log field `qMode` ∈ none | scalar_short | metadata_indexed.
#
#   3. New action family `preview`:
#        ?action_family=preview → [digest_send_preview]
#      DigestAuditLogCard chip strip adds "Preview". Action exact
#      filter still wins over action_family when both are supplied.
#
#   4. POST /api/admin/digest/preview writes a `digest_send_preview`
#      audit row after every successful send when
#      DIGEST_AUDIT_LOG_CRON_SENDS=1 (the existing Phase 8AD env
#      gate). Operators flip one flag to get per-recipient audit
#      coverage across cron + preview surfaces.
#
#   5. DigestAuditLogCard syncs filter state to the URL:
#        ?digest_audit_family=<family>
#        ?digest_audit_q=<term>
#        ?digest_audit_cursor=<iso>   (only when Load older clicked)
#      URL wins over localStorage on load. `family` persists in
#      localStorage (`venuerise:digest-audit-log:family:v1`) as a
#      fallback. `q` is URL-only by design — never persisted.
#      Reset button (visible when any filter is active) clears URL
#      params + localStorage family key. Uses router.replace so the
#      browser back button still exits the billing page on first
#      click.
#
#   Existing knobs still apply:
#     - OPERATOR_DIGEST_ENABLED=1 enables the operator-activity-digest cron.
#     - DIGEST_UNSUBSCRIBE_SECRET signs footer links.
#     - DIGEST_AUDIT_RETENTION_ENABLED=1 enables the weekly retention cron.
#     - DIGEST_AUDIT_RETENTION_DAYS sets the retention window.
#     - DIGEST_AUDIT_RETENTION_DRY_RUN=1 puts retention in preview mode.
#     - DIGEST_AUDIT_LOG_CRON_SENDS=1 logs cron AND preview sends as
#       digest_send_cron / digest_send_preview audit events.
# =============================================================================

# =============================================================================
# Phase 8AD — Audit log search/cursor + cron-fired toast + cron-send audit.
#
# One new env var, no new admin routes, no migrations. Three
# behavior changes on the existing digest audit surface plus an
# opt-in per-recipient cron-send audit:
#
#   1. GET /api/admin/digest/audit-events accepts:
#        ?q=<string, max 120>
#        ?occurred_before=<ISO datetime>
#        ?action_family=suppression|retention|cron|all
#
#      `q` ILIKE-searches `action`, `reason`, `target_email_masked`.
#      UUID columns (actor_user_id, target_user_id) are NOT in the
#      search allowlist — PostgREST's .or() can't cast and ILIKE on
#      a uuid column needs `::text`. Operators looking up a specific
#      user should use the `?target_user_id=` exact filter (indexed).
#
#      `occurred_before` is a strict-< descending cursor; JSON
#      response adds { next_cursor, has_more }; CSV adds
#      X-Has-More + X-Next-Cursor headers.
#
#      `action_family` is a server-side fan-out:
#        suppression -> suppression_remove | suppression_remove_noop
#                       | suppression_remove_all
#        retention   -> digest_retention_archive
#        cron        -> digest_send_cron       (Phase 8AD; opt-in)
#        all         -> no family filter
#      `action` exact-match wins over `action_family` when both are
#      supplied.
#
#   2. DigestAuditLogCard gains a search input (300ms debounce) +
#      Load older button. Active chip + search persist across the
#      cursor. Empty state distinguishes "no events ever" from
#      "no matches under the active filter". The chip strip now
#      uses `?action_family=` (single round-trip) instead of the
#      Phase 8AC client-side multi-fetch.
#
#   3. Optional per-recipient cron-send audit events. Default off:
#
# DIGEST_AUDIT_LOG_CRON_SENDS=0
#
#      When `1`, every successful `operator-activity-digest` cron
#      send writes one row to `digest_audit_events` via the
#      existing recordDigestAuditEvent helper:
#        actorKind: 'cron'
#        action:    'digest_send_cron'
#        targetUserId: <recipient>
#        targetEmailMasked: <masked>
#        metadata: { venue_id, event_count, cadence, weekly_day,
#                    outbound_message_id, send_kind: 'cron' }
#      Best-effort write — audit failures NEVER fail the cron send.
#      Default off because a busy multi-venue deployment can produce
#      high row volume (one per recipient per day).
#
#   4. DigestCronHealthCard now also surfaces an auto-dismissing
#      inline "Digest cron just ran." notice on every
#      `venuerise:digest-cron-fired` CustomEvent dispatched by
#      RealtimeDigestSendsLayer. Auto-clears after ~4s; card-local
#      (no global toast dependency).
#
#   Existing knobs still apply:
#     - OPERATOR_DIGEST_ENABLED=1 enables the operator-activity-digest cron.
#     - DIGEST_UNSUBSCRIBE_SECRET signs footer links.
#     - DIGEST_AUDIT_RETENTION_ENABLED=1 enables the weekly retention cron.
#     - DIGEST_AUDIT_RETENTION_DAYS sets the retention window.
#     - DIGEST_AUDIT_RETENTION_DRY_RUN=1 puts retention in preview mode.
#
# Phase 8AD — optional cron-send audit events
# Phase 8AE — also gates preview-send audit events (digest_send_preview)
# DIGEST_AUDIT_LOG_CRON_SENDS=0
# =============================================================================

# =============================================================================
# Phase 8AC — Show archived toggle + cron health realtime + digest audit events.
#
# One new env var (retention dry-run), one new migration (017
# digest_audit_events), one new admin endpoint
# (/api/admin/digest/audit-events). Behavior summary:
#
#   1. Migration 017 introduces public.digest_audit_events. Append-
#      only audit log for operator + cron actions against the digest
#      system. RLS: SELECT for owner/admin via has_venue_role;
#      writes are service-role only.
#
#   2. lib/billing/digest-audit-events.ts ships recordDigestAuditEvent()
#      — best-effort writer that NEVER throws. Used by:
#        - /api/admin/digest/suppressions/remove        (per-row action)
#        - /api/admin/digest/suppressions/remove-all    (bulk summary + per-target)
#        - lib/jobs/functions/digest-audit-retention.ts (per-venue summary)
#
#   3. GET /api/admin/digest/audit-events surfaces the table with
#      JSON + CSV branches, cursor pagination (?occurred_before=),
#      action / actor_kind / target_user_id / since filters.
#      Rate-limit: admin:digest-audit-events:{userId}.
#
#   4. DigestAuditLogCard mounted on /dashboard/settings/billing.
#      Chips: All / Suppression / Retention. CSV export inherits
#      filter (single-action scope per family).
#
#   5. DigestAuditFeed gains a `Show archived` checkbox above the
#      chip strip. Persisted in localStorage
#      (`venuerise:digest-audit-feed:include-archived:v1`).
#      Threads ?include_archived=true into JSON / Load older / CSV
#      URLs. Archived rows render with an extra slate "Archived" tag.
#
#   6. RealtimeDigestSendsLayer dispatches
#      `venuerise:digest-cron-fired` CustomEvent on cron-tagged
#      INSERTs. DigestCronHealthCard listens and refetches without
#      a full page reload. Existing suppression-refresh dispatch
#      from Phase 8AA is unchanged.
#
#   7. Retention cron supports a dry-run mode that previews
#      candidates without mutating:
#
# DIGEST_AUDIT_RETENTION_DRY_RUN=0
#
#      When ENABLED=1 AND DRY_RUN=1, the cron returns
#      { dry_run: true, candidate_count, sample_ids, retention_days }
#      and skips both the metadata mutation AND the audit-event
#      write. Use it to validate a retention window change before
#      flipping live.
#
#   PII posture:
#     - target_email_masked is always pre-masked at write time;
#       digest_audit_events never stores raw emails.
#     - audit helper logs metadata KEYS only, not values.
#     - CSV emits a compact JSON serialization of metadata, but
#       writers (suppression / retention) only put structural fields
#       (route, counts, retention_days) so no PII leaks downstream.
#
#   Existing knobs still apply:
#     - OPERATOR_DIGEST_ENABLED=1 enables the operator-activity-digest cron.
#     - DIGEST_UNSUBSCRIBE_SECRET signs footer links.
#     - DIGEST_AUDIT_RETENTION_ENABLED=1 enables the weekly retention cron.
#     - DIGEST_AUDIT_RETENTION_DAYS sets the retention window.
#
# Phase 8AC — digest audit retention dry-run
# DIGEST_AUDIT_RETENTION_DRY_RUN=0
# =============================================================================

# =============================================================================
# Phase 8AB — Digest audit retention + cron health + bulk suppression remove.
#
# Two new env vars (both optional with safe defaults), plus three new
# operational surfaces on /dashboard/settings/billing.
#
#   1. Weekly Inngest cron `digest-audit-retention` (schedule
#      `0 9 * * 1`, Monday 9am UTC). Soft-archives outbound digest
#      rows older than the configured window. Env-gated:
#
# DIGEST_AUDIT_RETENTION_ENABLED=0
# DIGEST_AUDIT_RETENTION_DAYS=365
#
#      When ENABLED is unset / not '1', the cron short-circuits to
#      `{ skipped: true, reason: 'disabled' }`. DAYS defaults to 365;
#      clamped to [30, 3650]. Soft-delete only: rows are tagged with
#      `metadata.digest_archived='true'` + `digest_archived_at` +
#      `digest_archived_reason='retention_policy'` +
#      `digest_retention_days=<N>`. NEVER deletes.
#      Batch cap: 500 rows per run; weekly cadence gives ~52 ticks
#      of headroom for any backlog.
#
#   2. GET /api/admin/digest/sends accepts a new query param:
#        ?include_archived=true|false   (default false)
#      Default path excludes rows where
#      `metadata->>'digest_archived'='true'`. Set the flag to surface
#      archived rows for forensic queries. JSON response carries a
#      new boolean `archived` field per item; CSV adds an `archived`
#      column.
#
#   3. New admin endpoint GET /api/admin/digest/cron-health surfaces
#      a delivery-derived health indicator:
#        { venue_id, ok, last_run_at, lag_minutes,
#          status: 'ok'|'stale'|'no_data',
#          expected_schedule: 'daily 8am UTC',
#          last_summary: { status, event_count, recipient_user_id,
#                          cadence, weekly_day } }
#      Status logic: `ok` if last cron row < 30h; `stale` if older;
#      `no_data` if zero cron rows in the last 72h.
#      Caveat: a venue with no tour activity in the last 24h won't
#      get a digest (the cron skips zero-event venues), so 'no_data'
#      can be normal. The DigestCronHealthCard surfaces this caveat
#      inline.
#      Rate-limit: admin:digest-cron-health:{userId}.
#
#   4. New admin endpoint POST /api/admin/digest/suppressions/remove-all
#      Body: { venue_id?, reason? (max 240) }
#      Iterates the venue's owner/admin members (cap 10),
#      re-resolves emails server-side, deletes matching rows from
#      email_suppressions. Returns:
#        { success, venue_id, removed_count,
#          details: [{ user_id, email_masked, removed, reason? }] }
#      reason ∈ 'email_missing' | 'not_suppressed' | 'unexpected_error'.
#      DigestSuppressionsCallout shows a single "Remove all
#      suppressions" button when items.length >= 3.
#      Rate-limit: admin:digest-suppressions-remove-all:{userId}.
#
#   5. DigestAuditFeed gains search highlights — case-insensitive
#      <mark> wrapping over visible cells (send_kind, status,
#      recipient_email, cadence, weekly_day). Uses String.indexOf
#      with lowercased halves; no regex injection, no
#      dangerouslySetInnerHTML. Hidden metadata isn't highlighted
#      because it isn't rendered.
#
#   Existing knobs still apply:
#     - OPERATOR_DIGEST_ENABLED=1 enables the operator-activity-digest cron.
#     - DIGEST_UNSUBSCRIBE_SECRET signs footer links.
#
# Phase 8AB — digest audit retention
# DIGEST_AUDIT_RETENTION_ENABLED=0
# DIGEST_AUDIT_RETENTION_DAYS=365
# =============================================================================

# =============================================================================
# Phase 8AA — Suppression removal + digest audit search + realtime callout refresh.
#
# No new env vars. Reuses email_suppressions, venue_members,
# Supabase Auth admin, and the existing digest endpoint stack.
# Behavior summary:
#
#   1. New admin endpoint POST /api/admin/digest/suppressions/remove
#      Body:
#        { venue_id?: uuid, user_id: uuid, reason?: string<=240 }
#      Server re-resolves the target's email from venue_members +
#      Supabase Auth admin — client never sends the address.
#      Role-gates target to owner/admin (404 on miss). Deletes from
#      email_suppressions by resolved email. Returns:
#        { success, removed: true|false, venue_id, user_id, email_masked }
#      On "removed: false" carries `reason: 'not_suppressed'`.
#      Rate-limit: admin:digest-suppressions-remove:{userId}.
#      Audit: structured log line `admin.digest_suppression_remove.completed`
#      with requestId / venueId / targetUserId / removed flag / optional
#      operator-supplied `reason`. No new audit table.
#
#   2. DigestSuppressionsCallout gains a "Remove suppression" button
#      on each row. Confirms via window.confirm before POSTing; per-
#      row state so two concurrent removals don't stomp each other.
#      On success, refetches and the row disappears (callout
#      auto-hides when the list is empty).
#
#   3. GET /api/admin/digest/sends accepts a new query param:
#        ?q=<string, max 120>
#      Short queries (1-2 chars) match a narrow allowlist:
#        status, provider, error, to_address,
#        metadata->>tour_digest_send_kind
#      Full queries (3+ chars) widen to:
#        + metadata->>tour_digest_cadence
#        + metadata->>tour_digest_weekly_day
#        + metadata->>tour_digest_recipient_user_id
#        + metadata->>tour_digest_manual_initiator_user_id
#      Applies to both JSON and CSV branches. Composes with the
#      Phase 8Z `occurred_before` cursor so Load older preserves
#      the search.
#      Internal-only: ILIKE on `to_address` is allowed for admin
#      search (operator may remember the local-part of an address);
#      raw email NEVER appears in responses.
#
#   4. DigestAuditFeed gets a search input above the chip strip,
#      300ms debounced, with the short-query hint banner from
#      Phase 8U status-events:
#        "Searching core fields only. Type 3+ characters for fuller
#         metadata matching."
#      Top-3-recipient summary entries become clickable to pin the
#      feed to that recipient's sends (sets the existing
#      recipient_user_id endpoint filter). Active pin renders as a
#      small blue chip with a × clear button.
#
#   5. RealtimeDigestSendsLayer dispatches a browser CustomEvent
#      (`venuerise:digest-suppression-refresh`) whenever a new
#      outbound digest row arrives with status='suppressed'.
#      DigestSuppressionsCallout listens via window.addEventListener
#      and refetches — surfaces a fresh bounce without a page
#      reload. Cleanup on unmount.
#
#   Existing knobs still apply:
#     - OPERATOR_DIGEST_ENABLED=1 enables the digest cron.
#     - DIGEST_UNSUBSCRIBE_SECRET signs footer links.
# =============================================================================

# =============================================================================
# Phase 8Z — Digest audit pagination + realtime + suppression triage.
#
# No new env vars. Reuses Supabase Realtime, the existing
# outbound_messages / email_suppressions tables, and the Phase 8Y
# digest endpoint stack.
#
#   1. GET /api/admin/digest/sends accepts a new query param:
#        ?occurred_before=<ISO datetime>
#      JSON response shape becomes additive:
#        { items, next_cursor, has_more }
#      CSV branch adds response headers:
#        X-Has-More: true|false
#        X-Next-Cursor: <iso>  (only when has_more=true)
#      Cursor uses strict `<` so chained pages never duplicate the
#      boundary row. Hard limit 200 unchanged.
#
#   2. DigestAuditFeed gains a "Load older" button that appears when
#      the most recent fetch returned has_more=true. Clicking
#      appends the next page to the visible list; existing rows
#      stay put (no full reload). Filter change resets pagination.
#
#   3. RealtimeDigestSendsLayer subscribes to outbound_messages
#      INSERTs filtered by venue_id and narrows in-handler to digest
#      rows (related_table='tour_status_events' AND
#      metadata.tour_digest_send_kind present). Surfaces "New digest
#      send recorded" toast per event + trailing 1000ms debounce on
#      router.refresh() so cron bursts coalesce into one rebuild.
#
#      PREREQ: outbound_messages must be in the supabase_realtime
#      publication. NOT enabled by default (migration 001 only
#      publishes leads / messages / conversations / tours). Apply
#      out-of-band (not a migration file):
#
#        alter publication supabase_realtime
#        add table public.outbound_messages;
#
#      Without the publication entry the channel subscribes
#      successfully but receives no events. See RUNBOOK
#      "Digest audit feed not updating live".
#
#   4. New admin endpoint GET /api/admin/digest/suppressions
#      intersects venue owner/admin members with the global
#      email_suppressions table (migration 003) and returns rows
#      that belong to receivers of this venue's digest. Response:
#        { venue_id, items: [{ user_id, role, email_masked,
#                              reason, created_at }] }
#      reason ∈ bounce | complaint | manual | unknown. The
#      'unsubscribe' suppression kind is deliberately collapsed to
#      'unknown' so the callout doesn't conflate digest-cadence
#      opt-out with delivery suppression.
#      Rate-limit: admin:digest-suppressions:{userId}.
#      PII posture: raw emails NEVER returned or logged.
#
#   5. DigestSuppressionsCallout amber banner mounted on
#      /dashboard/settings/billing above DigestAuditFeed. Renders
#      NOTHING when no admin emails are suppressed (happy path);
#      otherwise lists masked emails + reasons. No remove action
#      yet — direct operators to the Resend dashboard or the
#      existing admin suppressions surface.
#
#   Known limitations:
#     - Realtime INSERT events may fire BEFORE the Resend webhook
#       updates the row from `queued` → `delivered`. The audit feed
#       row will repaint as `queued` first, then `delivered` a few
#       seconds later when /api/resend/webhook lands.
#     - Suppression callout uses email lookup via Supabase Auth
#       admin (same as the picker). A venue with > 10 admins drops
#       the 11th+ from this surface too — matches the cron fan-out
#       cap.
#
#   Existing knobs still apply:
#     - OPERATOR_DIGEST_ENABLED=1 enables the digest cron.
#     - DIGEST_UNSUBSCRIBE_SECRET signs unsubscribe + resubscribe
#       footer links.
# =============================================================================

# =============================================================================
# Phase 8Y — Digest member picker + send audit feed + respect_cadence manual.
#
# No new env vars. Reuses Supabase Auth admin for member email
# resolution, DIGEST_UNSUBSCRIBE_SECRET (Phase 8S) for footer links,
# and the existing Resend pipeline. Behavior summary:
#
#   1. New admin endpoint GET /api/admin/digest/members lists owner +
#      admin members of the venue + their resolved auth.users.email.
#      Bounded concurrency (5) email lookups, capped at 10 members
#      per venue (same cap as the cron's fan-out). Powers the
#      DigestPreferencesCard recipient picker so manual sends can
#      target another admin without typing a UUID. Rate-limit:
#      admin:digest-members:{userId}.
#
#   2. New admin endpoint GET /api/admin/digest/sends — operator
#      audit feed over outbound_messages digest rows. Query params:
#        venue_id?, send_kind? = cron|preview|manual|all,
#        recipient_user_id?, since? = ISO datetime,
#        limit? (1..200, default 50), format? = json|csv
#      JSON returns { items: [...] }. CSV returns a UTF-8 BOM file
#      with explicit column allowlist. Recipient email is masked
#      (`y***@domain.com`); subject/body/full metadata never returned.
#      Rate-limit: admin:digest-sends:{userId}.
#
#   3. DigestAuditFeed card mounted on /dashboard/settings/billing
#      (admins/owners only) below DigestPreferencesCard. Shows the
#      last 25 sends with kind chips + CSV export link. Empty state
#      "No digest sends recorded yet."
#
#   4. DigestPreferencesCard recipient picker:
#        - defaults to current user
#        - collapses to "Sending to you" when the venue has only one
#          receivable owner/admin member
#        - renders a `<select>` listing every owner/admin member when
#          multiple exist; members with no resolvable email are
#          disabled in the select so the operator sees the gap
#      + new "Honor selected recipient's cadence preference" checkbox
#      below the picker. When checked, POSTs `respect_cadence: true`
#      to /api/admin/digest/send.
#
#   5. Manual-send endpoint formalized the respect_cadence skip
#      response shape:
#        { success: true, sent: false, skipped: true,
#          reason: 'off' | 'weekly_wrong_day' }
#      The card renders amber inline status:
#        off              → "Skipped because this recipient's cadence is off."
#        weekly_wrong_day → "Skipped because this recipient's weekly digest is not scheduled today."
#
#   Known limitation: the audit feed reads outbound_messages, not the
#   Resend webhook directly. Status may lag delivery by a few seconds
#   until /api/resend/webhook updates the row.
#
#   Existing knobs still apply:
#     - OPERATOR_DIGEST_ENABLED=1 enables the digest cron.
#     - DIGEST_UNSUBSCRIBE_SECRET signs unsubscribe + resubscribe
#       footer links.
# =============================================================================

# =============================================================================
# Phase 8X — Digest footer links + manual digest send + send-kind 'manual'.
#
# No new env vars. Reuses DIGEST_UNSUBSCRIBE_SECRET (Phase 8S) to sign
# every footer link (both unsubscribe + resubscribe). Behavior summary:
#
#   1. Digest emails (cron, preview, manual) now render an explicit
#      "Manage your digest preferences" footer link pointing at
#      /dashboard/settings/billing, in both plaintext and HTML bodies.
#
#   2. Cron sends include the venue-level unsubscribe link only — the
#      resubscribe link is omitted because a recipient receiving a
#      cron digest has effective cadence 'daily' or 'weekly' (off
#      would have been skipped upstream), so the re-enable link would
#      just add clutter.
#
#   3. Preview + manual sends include BOTH unsubscribe + resubscribe
#      footer links so an operator can QA the full preference loop in
#      one click.
#
#   4. When DIGEST_UNSUBSCRIBE_SECRET is missing, all footer links are
#      omitted across every surface and a single structured warn fires
#      once per process (shared flag across cron / preview / manual).
#      Emails still send.
#
#   5. New admin endpoint POST /api/admin/digest/send fans out a single
#      digest email to a target owner/admin member of the venue.
#      Differences from preview:
#        - can target ANOTHER owner/admin via body.user_id (defaults
#          to caller)
#        - outbound row is tagged tour_digest_send_kind='manual'
#        - optional body.respect_cadence (default false): when true,
#          honors the recipient's cadence preference and returns
#          { sent: false, skipped: true, reason } on a skip
#        - always bypasses cron idempotency
#      Cross-tenant venue access re-checks requireVenueRole(ADMIN_ROLES);
#      a non-admin target collapses to 404 so the route can't enumerate
#      per-role distribution. Cross-user sends omit `sent_to` in the
#      response to prevent email enumeration.
#      Rate-limit key: admin:digest-send:{userId}.
#
#   6. tour_digest_send_kind now formally accepts:
#        'cron'    — scheduled cron send (Phase 8W)
#        'preview' — POST /api/admin/digest/preview (Phase 8W)
#        'manual'  — POST /api/admin/digest/send (Phase 8X)
#      The cron's per-recipient idempotency probe filters strictly on
#      send_kind='cron'; preview + manual rows are never dedupe
#      candidates.
#
#   Existing knobs still apply:
#     - OPERATOR_DIGEST_ENABLED=1 enables the digest cron.
#     - DIGEST_UNSUBSCRIBE_SECRET signs unsubscribe, resubscribe, and
#       cron/preview/manual footer links. Rotating invalidates every
#       in-flight token of either kind across every surface.
# =============================================================================

# =============================================================================
# Phase 8V — Digest preview + recipient batching + member backfill.
#
# Three additive surfaces, two of them env-flag-free:
#
#   1. POST /api/admin/digest/preview sends a sample digest to the
#      caller's auth.users.email right now. Bypasses cadence +
#      idempotency. Outbound row is tagged with
#      `metadata.tour_digest_preview = 'true'` so the cron's per-
#      recipient idempotency probe ignores it on the next 8am UTC
#      tick. Uses the existing Resend / RESEND_API_KEY config; no
#      new env vars.
#
#   2. `findDigestRecipients` (digest cron) now resolves emails via
#      bounded concurrency 5 + Promise.allSettled instead of serial
#      auth lookups. Same return shape; per-member failures isolated.
#      No env knob.
#
#   3. Manual Inngest backfill `seed-member-digest-preferences` writes
#      explicit `digest_cadence='daily'` onto owner/admin
#      venue_members rows whose metadata lacks one. Caps at 1000 rows
#      per run; idempotent. Trigger via the Inngest dashboard with the
#      event `admin/member-digest-preferences.backfill`.
# =============================================================================
# SEED_MEMBER_DIGEST=0
# =============================================================================
# NEXT_PUBLIC_DEMO_BUTTON=0

# =============================================================================
# Phase 7D — billing subscription gate.
#
# When BILLING_GATE_ENABLED=1, write routes (POST/PATCH/DELETE on leads,
# tours, AI, team invites) return HTTP 402 `subscription_required` unless
# the caller's venue has a `trialing` or `active` subscription row.
#
# Default is DISABLED so a staged rollout can ship the dashboard banner +
# settings/billing page WITHOUT changing API behavior, then flip the gate
# on once Stripe billing is live and trial flows have been validated.
#
# Reads, dashboard loading, /api/billing/checkout, /api/billing/portal,
# team-accept-invite, onboarding-create-workspace, the public widget, and
# Stripe/Resend webhooks are NEVER gated.
#
# Toggle: set the value to exactly the string "1" to enable. Anything else
# (unset, empty, "true", "yes") leaves the gate disabled.
# =============================================================================
# BILLING_GATE_ENABLED=0

# =============================================================================
# Phase 7B — local/CI smoke script env vars (NOT Vercel runtime vars).
# These are read by `scripts/smoke-prod.mjs` and `scripts/load-widget.mjs`
# when you run them from a laptop or CI runner against a deployed app.
# Do NOT set them in Vercel — they have no runtime effect there.
# =============================================================================

# scripts/smoke-prod.mjs — end-to-end smoke. Hits health/readiness, signs in
# the test user via Supabase password grant, drives a widget POST, polls
# Supabase REST for downstream rows, cleans up its own rows on success.
# SMOKE_APP_URL=https://staging.venuerise.com
# SMOKE_SUPABASE_URL=https://xxx.supabase.co
# SMOKE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=eyJ...
# SMOKE_SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=eyJ...
# SMOKE_TEST_USER_EMAIL=smoke-owner@example.com
# SMOKE_TEST_USER_PASSWORD=replace-me
# SMOKE_EXISTING_VENUE_ID=          # optional — skip venue lookup
# SMOKE_SKIP_ONBOARDING=0           # reserved; cookie-auth onboarding is skipped by default
# SMOKE_TIMEOUT_MS=60000            # overall HTTP timeout cap

# scripts/load-widget.mjs — widget POST load smoke. Reports p50/p90/p95/p99/max.
# LOAD_APP_URL=https://staging.venuerise.com
# LOAD_VENUE_ID=<uuid>
# LOAD_CONCURRENCY=10
# LOAD_TOTAL=30
# LOAD_EXPECT_RATE_LIMIT=0          # 1 to tolerate 429s
# LOAD_SUPABASE_URL=                # optional — enables row cleanup
# LOAD_SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=   # optional — enables row cleanup

# Phase 7D — when set to "1", the smoke script asserts that a gated write
# route returns HTTP 402 (subscription_required) and that the widget still
# returns 201. Leave at 0 in environments where the gate isn't enforced.
# SMOKE_EXPECT_BILLING_GATE=0

# =============================================================================
# Phase 7E — billing QA scripts.
#
# Two local/CI utilities for verifying the subscription gate. See
# docs/BILLING-QA.md for the full walk-through.
#
# scripts/billing-gate-matrix.mjs — probes representative routes against a
# deployed app and asserts each response matches the gate matrix. Set
# BILLING_MATRIX_EXPECT_GATE=1 in environments where you flipped the gate on.
# BILLING_MATRIX_APP_URL=https://staging.venuerise.com
# BILLING_MATRIX_SUPABASE_URL=https://xxx.supabase.co
# BILLING_MATRIX_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=eyJ...
# BILLING_MATRIX_SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=eyJ...
# BILLING_MATRIX_TEST_USER_EMAIL=smoke-owner@example.com
# BILLING_MATRIX_TEST_USER_PASSWORD=replace-me
# BILLING_MATRIX_VENUE_ID=<uuid>
# BILLING_MATRIX_EXPECT_GATE=0
# BILLING_MATRIX_CLEANUP=1

# scripts/seed-subscription-state.mjs — STAGING / LOCAL ONLY.
# Inserts a synthetic subscription row tagged metadata.source='billing_gate_test'
# so the QA loop can move a venue between trialing / active / past_due /
# canceled / incomplete / none without round-tripping through Stripe. Never
# run against production data.
# SEED_SUBSCRIPTION_SUPABASE_URL=https://xxx.supabase.co
# SEED_SUBSCRIPTION_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=eyJ...
# SEED_SUBSCRIPTION_VENUE_ID=<uuid>
# SEED_SUBSCRIPTION_STATUS=trialing
# SEED_SUBSCRIPTION_TRIAL_DAYS=14
